1
00:00:00,659 --> 00:00:04,020
section 2 chapter 7.

2
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:08,878
Winston had woken up with his eyes full of tears. Julia rolled sleepily against

3
00:00:08,878 --> 00:00:13,560
him, murmuring something that might have been 'What's the matter?'

4
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,680
'I dreamed he began, and stopped short.

5
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:19,319
It was too complex to be put into words.

6
00:00:19,319 --> 00:00:22,860
There was the dream itself, and there was a memory connected with it that had

7
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,340
swung into his mind in the few seconds after waking.

8
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:31,020
He lay back with his eyes shut, still sodden in the atmosphere of the dream.

9
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:34,797
It was a vast, luminous dream in which his whole life seemed to stretch out

10
00:00:34,797 --> 00:00:38,700
before him like a landscape on a summer evening after rain.

11
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:42,599
It had all occurred inside the glass paperweight, but the surface of the glass

12
00:00:42,599 --> 00:00:46,500
was the dome of the sky, and inside the dome everything was flooded with clear

13
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:50,700
soft light in which one could see into interminable distances.

14
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:55,439
The dream had also been comprehended by— —indeed, in some sense it had consisted in—

15
00:00:55,439 --> 00:01:00,419
—a gesture of the arm made by his mother, and made again 30 years later by the

16
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:04,200
Jewish woman he had seen on the news film, trying to shelter the small boy

17
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:09,297
from the bullets, before the helicopter blew them both to pieces.

18
00:01:09,297 --> 00:01:14,878
'Do you know,' he said, 'that until this moment I believed I had murdered my mother?'

19
00:01:14,878 --> 00:01:18,957
'Why did you murder her?' said Julia, almost asleep.

20
00:01:18,957 --> 00:01:22,740
'I didn't murder her. Not physically.'

21
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:26,939
In the dream he had remembered his last glimpse of his mother, and within a few

22
00:01:26,939 --> 00:01:30,180
moments of waking the cluster of small events surrounding it had all come back.

23
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:35,700
It was a memory that he must have deliberately pushed out of his consciousness over many years.

24
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:39,060
He was not certain of the date, but he could not have been less than 10 years

25
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:42,360
old, possibly 12 when it had happened.

26
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:46,680
His father had disappeared sometime earlier; how much earlier, he could not remember.

27
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:51,000
He remembered better the rackety, uneasy circumstances of the time:

28
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,200
the periodical panics about Air Raids and the sheltering in Tube stations, the

29
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,218
piles of rubble everywhere, the unintelligible proclamations posted at

30
00:01:59,218 --> 00:02:03,419
street corners, the gangs of youths in shirts all the same color the enormous

31
00:02:03,419 --> 00:02:08,400
cues outside the bakeries, the intermittent machine gun fire in the distance—

32
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:12,060
—above all, the fact that there was never enough to eat.

33
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:16,137
He remembered long afternoons spent with other boys in scrounging round dustbins

34
00:02:16,137 --> 00:02:20,218
and rubbish heaps, picking out the ribs of cabbage leaves, potato peelings,

35
00:02:20,218 --> 00:02:25,797
sometimes even scraps of stale bread crust from which they carefully scraped away the cinders;

36
00:02:25,797 --> 00:02:29,099
and also in waiting for the passing of trucks which traveled over a certain

37
00:02:29,099 --> 00:02:33,240
route and were known to carry cattle feed, and which, when they jolted over the bad

38
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:38,419
patches in the road, sometimes spilled a few fragments of oil cake

39
00:02:38,637 --> 00:02:42,540
When his father disappeared, his mother did not show any surprise or any violent

40
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:45,718
grief, but the sudden change came over her.

41
00:02:45,718 --> 00:02:49,080
She seemed to have become completely spiritless.

42
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:53,878
It was evident even to Winston that she was waiting for something that she knew must happen.

43
00:02:53,878 --> 00:02:58,919
She did everything that was needed— —cooked, washed, mended, made the bed, swept

44
00:02:58,919 --> 00:03:03,240
the floor, dusted the mantelpiece— —always very slowly and with a curious

45
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:08,218
lack of superfluous motion, like an artist's sleigh figure moving of its own accord

46
00:03:08,218 --> 00:03:12,659
a large shapely body seemed to relapse naturally into stillness.

47
00:03:12,659 --> 00:03:17,159
For hours at a time she would sit almost immobile on the bed, nursing his young

48
00:03:17,159 --> 00:03:23,700
sister, a tiny, ailing, very silent child of two or three, with a face made Simeon by thinness.

49
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:27,419
Very occasionally she would take Winston in her arms and press him against her

50
00:03:27,419 --> 00:03:30,419
for a long time without saying anything.

51
00:03:30,419 --> 00:03:33,900
He was aware, in spite of his youthfulness and selfishness, that this

52
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:38,218
was somehow connected with the never mentioned thing that was about to happen.

53
00:03:38,218 --> 00:03:42,659
He remembered the room where they lived, a dark, close- smelling room that seemed

54
00:03:42,659 --> 00:03:45,659
half-filled by a bed with a white counterpane.

55
00:03:45,659 --> 00:03:49,919
There was a gas ring in the fender, and a shelf where food was kept, and on the

56
00:03:49,919 --> 00:03:54,718
landing outside there was a brown earthenware sink, common to several rooms.

57
00:03:54,718 --> 00:03:58,500
He remembered his mother's statuesque body bending over the gas ring to stir

58
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:00,599
at something in a saucepan.

59
00:04:00,599 --> 00:04:06,360
Above all he remembered his continuous hunger, and the fierce sordered battles at meal times

60
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:10,080
He would ask his mother naggingly, over and over again, why there was not more

61
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:14,878
food, he would shout and storm at her (he even remembered the tones of his voice,

62
00:04:14,878 --> 00:04:18,840
which was beginning to break prematurely and sometimes boomed in a peculiar way),

63
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:25,137
or he would attempt a sniveling note of pathos in his efforts to get more than his share.

64
00:04:25,137 --> 00:04:28,797
His mother was quite ready to give him more than his share. She took it for

65
00:04:28,797 --> 00:04:33,180
granted that he, 'the boy', should have the biggest portion; but however much she

66
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:35,637
gave him he invariably demanded more.

67
00:04:35,637 --> 00:04:39,060
At every meal she would beseech him not to be selfish and to remember that his

68
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:43,137
little sister was sick and also needed food, but it was no use.

69
00:04:43,137 --> 00:04:46,797
He would cry out with rage when she stopped laggling he would try to wrench

70
00:04:46,797 --> 00:04:51,659
the saucepan and spoon out of her hands, he would grab bits from his sister's plate.

71
00:04:51,659 --> 00:04:55,137
He knew that he was starving the other two, but he could not help it; he even

72
00:04:55,137 --> 00:04:57,360
felt that he had a right to do it.

73
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:00,900
The Glamorous hunger in his belly seemed to justify him.

74
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:05,279
Between meals, if his mother did not stand guard, he was constantly pilfering

75
00:05:05,279 --> 00:05:08,159
at the wretched store of food on the shelf.

76
00:05:08,159 --> 00:05:11,218
One day a chocolate ration was issued.

77
00:05:11,218 --> 00:05:14,580
There had been no such issue for weeks or months past.

78
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:18,297
He remembered quite clearly that precious little morsel of chocolate.

79
00:05:18,297 --> 00:05:22,020
It was a two ounce slab (they still talked about answers in those days)

80
00:05:22,020 --> 00:05:24,180
between the three of them.

81
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:27,900
It was obvious that a door to be divided into three equal parts.

82
00:05:27,900 --> 00:05:31,319
Suddenly, as though you were listening to somebody else, Winston heard himself

83
00:05:31,319 --> 00:05:35,400
demanding in a loud booming voice that he should be given the whole piece.

84
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,797
His mother told him not to be greedy.

85
00:05:37,797 --> 00:05:42,119
There was a long, nagging argument that went round and round, with shouts fines

86
00:05:42,119 --> 00:05:45,240
tears, remonstrances, bargainings.

87
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:48,840
His tiny sister, clinging to her mother with both hands, exactly like a baby

88
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:53,218
monkey, sat looking over her shoulder at him with large, mournful eyes.

89
00:05:53,218 --> 00:05:56,580
In the end his mother broke off three quarters of the chocolate and gave it to

90
00:05:56,580 --> 00:06:00,000
Winston, giving the other quarter to his sister.

91
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,218
The little girl took hold of it and looked at it dully, perhaps not knowing what it was.

92
00:06:05,218 --> 00:06:07,979
Winston stood watching her for a moment.

93
00:06:07,979 --> 00:06:11,637
Then with the sudden swift spring he had snatched to the piece of chocolate out

94
00:06:11,637 --> 00:06:14,819
of his sister's hand and was fleeing for the door.

95
00:06:14,819 --> 00:06:19,378
'Winston, Winston!' his mother called after him. 'Come back! Give your sister back her

96
00:06:19,378 --> 00:06:20,520
chocolate!'

97
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:23,520
He stopped, but did not come back.

98
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:26,400
His mother's anxious eyes were fixed on his face.

99
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,599
Even now he was thinking about the thing, he did not know what it was that was on

100
00:06:30,599 --> 00:06:31,919
the point of happening.

101
00:06:31,919 --> 00:06:36,957
His sister, conscious of having been robbed of something, had set up a feeble whale

102
00:06:36,957 --> 00:06:41,580
His mother drew her arm around the child and pressed its face against her breast.

103
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:45,840
Something in the gesture told him that his sister was dying. He turned and fled

104
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:50,360
down the stairs, with the chocolate growing sticky in his hand.

105
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,740
He never saw his mother again.

106
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:56,819
After he had devoured the chocolate he felt somewhat ashamed of himself and

107
00:06:56,819 --> 00:07:01,259
hung about in the streets for several hours, until hunger drove him home.

108
00:07:01,259 --> 00:07:04,137
When he came back his mother had disappeared.

109
00:07:04,137 --> 00:07:07,080
This was already becoming normal at that time.

110
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:10,860
Nothing was gone from the room except his mother and his sister. They had not

111
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:13,979
taken any clothes, not even his mother's overcoat.

112
00:07:13,979 --> 00:07:18,119
To this day he did not know with any certainty that his mother was dead.

113
00:07:18,119 --> 00:07:22,680
It was perfectly possible that she had merely been sent to a forced labor camp.

114
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:26,520
As for his sister, she might have been removed, like Winston himself, to one of

115
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:27,979
the colonies for homeless children

116
00:07:27,979 --> 00:07:29,819
(Reclamation centers they were called)

117
00:07:29,819 --> 00:07:32,939
which had grown up as a result of the civil war;

118
00:07:32,939 --> 00:07:36,000
or she might have been sent to the labor camp along with his mother,

119
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,779
or simply left somewhere or other to die.

120
00:07:39,779 --> 00:07:43,740
The dream was still vivid in his mind, especially the enveloping, protecting

121
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:48,060
gesture of the arm in which its whole meaning seemed to be contained.

122
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:51,419
His mind went back to another dream of two months ago.

123
00:07:51,419 --> 00:07:55,378
Exactly as his mother had sat on the dingy white quilted bed, with the child

124
00:07:55,378 --> 00:08:00,119
clinging to her, so she had sat in the sunken ship, far underneath him and

125
00:08:00,119 --> 00:08:05,279
drowning deeper every minute, but still looking up at him through the darkening water.

126
00:08:05,279 --> 00:08:08,819
He told Julia the story of his mother's disappearance.

127
00:08:08,819 --> 00:08:14,060
Without opening her eyes she rolled over and settled herself into a more comfortable position.

128
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:19,137
'I expect you were a beastly little swine in those days,' she said indistinctly. 'All

129
00:08:19,137 --> 00:08:21,419
children are swine.'

130
00:08:21,419 --> 00:08:26,580
'Yes. But the real point of the story— From her breathing it was evident that

131
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:28,740
she was going off to sleep again.

132
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:31,740
He would have liked to continue talking about his mother.

133
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:35,218
He did not suppose, from what he could remember of her, that she had been an

134
00:08:35,218 --> 00:08:39,840
unusual woman, still less an intelligent one; and yet she had possessed a kind of

135
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:45,779
nobility, a kind of purity, simply because the standards that she obeyed were private ones.

136
00:08:45,779 --> 00:08:49,680
Her feelings were her own, and could not be altered from outside.

137
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:53,040
It would not have occurred to her that an action which is ineffectual thereby

138
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:54,419
becomes meaningless.

139
00:08:54,419 --> 00:08:58,797
If you loved someone, you loved him, and when you had nothing else to give, you

140
00:08:58,797 --> 00:09:00,360
still gave him love.

141
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:04,680
When the last of the chocolate was gone, his mother had clasped the child in her

142
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:09,659
arms. It was no use, it changed nothing, it did not produce more chocolate, it did

143
00:09:09,659 --> 00:09:15,000
not avert the child's death or her own; but it seemed natural to her to do it.

144
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,360
The refugee woman in the boat had also covered the little boy with her arm,

145
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:22,378
which was no more use against the bullets than a sheet of paper.

146
00:09:22,378 --> 00:09:25,500
The terrible thing that the Party had done was to persuade you that mere

147
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:29,400
impulses, mere feelings, were of no account, while at the same time robbing

148
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:32,400
you of all power over the material world.

149
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:36,718
When once you were in the grip of the Party, what you felt or did not feel, what

150
00:09:36,718 --> 00:09:40,200
you did or refrained from doing, made literally no difference.

151
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:43,860
Whatever happened you vanished, and neither you nor your actions were ever

152
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:48,119
heard of again. You were lifted clean out of the stream of history.

153
00:09:48,119 --> 00:09:51,119
And yet to the people of only two generations ago, this would not have

154
00:09:51,119 --> 00:09:55,080
seemed all important because they were not attempting to alter history.

155
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:58,439
They were governed by private loyalties which they did not question.

156
00:09:58,439 --> 00:10:02,279
What mattered were individual relationships, and a completely helpless

157
00:10:02,279 --> 00:10:08,580
gesture and embrace, a tear, a word spoken to a dying man, could have value in itself.

158
00:10:08,580 --> 00:10:12,180
The pearls had suddenly occurred to him, had remained in this condition.

159
00:10:12,180 --> 00:10:16,439
They were not loyal to a party or a country or an idea, they were loyal to

160
00:10:16,439 --> 00:10:17,580
one another.

161
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:21,297
For the first time in his life he did not despise the pros or think of them

162
00:10:21,297 --> 00:10:26,340
merely as an inert force which would one day spring to life and regenerate the world.

163
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:28,560
The pearls had stayed human.

164
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:32,819
They had not become hardened inside. They had held on to the primitive emotions

165
00:10:32,819 --> 00:10:36,240
which he himself had to relearn by conscious effort.

166
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:40,137
And in thinking this he remembered, without apparent relevance, how a few

167
00:10:40,137 --> 00:10:43,378
weeks ago he had seen a severed hand lying on the pavement and had kicked it

168
00:10:43,378 --> 00:10:46,259
into the gutter as though it had been a cabbage stalk

169
00:10:46,259 --> 00:10:48,599
The pros are human beings,' he said aloud.

170
00:10:48,599 --> 00:10:51,540
'We are not human.'

171
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:55,378
'Why not?' said Julia, who had woken up again.

172
00:10:55,378 --> 00:10:57,240
He thought for a little while.

173
00:10:57,240 --> 00:11:00,659
'Has it ever occurred to you,' he said, 'that the best thing for us to do would be

174
00:11:00,659 --> 00:11:05,878
simply to walk out of here before it's too late, and never see each other again?'

175
00:11:05,878 --> 00:11:09,779
'Yes, dear, it has occurred to me, several times. But I'm not going to do it, all the

176
00:11:09,779 --> 00:11:11,520
same.'

177
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:14,939
'We've been lucky,' he said, 'but it can't last much longer.

178
00:11:14,939 --> 00:11:18,900
You're young. You look normal and innocent. If you keep clear of people

179
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:22,560
like me, you might stay alive for another 50 years.'

180
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:27,419
'No. I've thought it all out. What you do, I'm going to do. And don't be too

181
00:11:27,419 --> 00:11:31,080
down-hearted I'm rather good at staying alive.'

182
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:36,060
'We may be together for another six months— —a year— —there's no knowing.

183
00:11:36,060 --> 00:11:40,797
At the end we're certain to be a part Do you realize how utterly alone we shall be?

184
00:11:40,797 --> 00:11:44,040
When once they get hold of us there will be nothing, literally nothing, that either

185
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:46,259
of us can do for the other.

186
00:11:46,259 --> 00:11:51,180
If I confess, they'll shoot you, and if I refuse to confess they'll shoot you just the same.

187
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:55,080
Nothing that I can do or say, or stop myself from saying, will put off your

188
00:11:55,080 --> 00:11:57,479
death for as much as five minutes.

189
00:11:57,479 --> 00:12:01,439
Neither of us will even know whether the other is alive or dead.

190
00:12:01,439 --> 00:12:04,319
We shall be utterly without power of any kind.

191
00:12:04,319 --> 00:12:08,040
The one thing that matters is that we shouldn't betray one another, although

192
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:11,279
even that can't make the slightest difference.'

193
00:12:11,279 --> 00:12:15,718
'If you mean confessing,' she said, 'we shall do that, right enough. Everybody always

194
00:12:15,718 --> 00:12:19,378
confesses. You can't help it. They torture you.'

195
00:12:19,378 --> 00:12:24,060
'I don't mean confessing. Confession is not betrayal. What you say or do doesn't

196
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:26,520
matter: only feelings matter.

197
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:30,540
If they could make me stop loving you— —that would be the real betrayal.'

198
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:32,340
She thought it over.

199
00:12:32,340 --> 00:12:37,319
'They can't do that,' she said finally. 'It's the one thing they can't do.

200
00:12:37,319 --> 00:12:41,819
They can make you say anything— anything— —but they can't make you believe it.

201
00:12:41,819 --> 00:12:44,400
They can't get inside you.'

202
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:48,840
'No,' he said a little more hopefully, 'no; that's quite true.

203
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:50,759
They can't get inside you.

204
00:12:50,759 --> 00:12:54,659
If you can feel that staying human is worthwhile even when it can't have any

205
00:12:54,659 --> 00:12:58,740
result whatever, you've beaten them.'

206
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:01,860
He thought of the telescreen with its never sleeping ear.

207
00:13:01,860 --> 00:13:06,419
They could spy upon unite and day, but if you kept your head you could still outwit them.

208
00:13:06,419 --> 00:13:10,200
With all their cleverness they had never mastered the secret of finding out what

209
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:13,797
another human being was thinking. Perhaps that was less true when you were

210
00:13:13,797 --> 00:13:15,540
actually in their hands.

211
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:20,580
One did not know what happened inside the Ministry of Love, but it was possible to guess:

212
00:13:20,580 --> 00:13:25,378
tortures, drugs, delicate instruments that registered your nervous reactions,

213
00:13:25,378 --> 00:13:30,200
gradual wearing down by sleeplessness and solitude and persistent questioning.

214
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:35,340
Facts, at any rate, could not be kept hidden there could be tracks done by inquiry

215
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:37,979
they could be squeezed out of you by torture.

216
00:13:37,979 --> 00:13:42,297
But if the object was not to stay alive but to stay human, what difference did it

217
00:13:42,297 --> 00:13:43,860
ultimately make?

218
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:47,159
They could not alter your feelings: for that matter you could not alter them

219
00:13:47,159 --> 00:13:49,319
yourself, even if you wanted to.

220
00:13:49,319 --> 00:13:53,819
They could lay bare in the utmost detail everything that you had done or said or thought;

221
00:13:53,819 --> 00:13:59,000
but the inner heart, whose workings were mysterious even to yourself, remained impregnable
